<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1935 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Aegukga</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ko">애국가</bdi> / <bdi lang="ko-Latn">Aegukga</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>South Korea's national anthem. Its words are from the 1890s; Ahn Eak-tai wrote its tune in 1935 so Koreans would have a melody of their own.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (attributed by various theories to Yun Ch'iho, Ahn Chang-ho or Min Young-hwan)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ahn Eak-tai</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Koreans under Japanese rule and in exile; later the Republic of Korea</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Korea</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1890s; music 1935; national anthem from 1948</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words of unknown authorship (1890s); music by Ahn Eak-tai (1935; he died in 1965), whose family gave all rights to the South Korean government in 2005)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegukga">Aegukga</a>&quot; means &quot;patriotic song&quot;. The word can name any song of love for one's country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> still has more than ten other songs called &quot;aegukga&quot;, but in general use it means the national anthem of South Korea. Its words date back to the 1890s. Who wrote them is not settled: theories name Yun Ch'iho, Ahn Chang-ho or Min Young-hwan, but the National Institute of Korean History holds that the author remains unknown.</p><p>The words were first sung to the tune of the Scottish song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a>&quot;, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> as &quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Farewell&quot;. In that form it was sung before the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, including by dissidents while Korea was under Japanese rule. The composer Ahn Eak-tai (1906–1965) first met the words during the March 1st Movement of 1919. Regretting that they were sung to a Scottish folk tune, he decided that a Korean should compose their melody, and he did so in 1935. His version was adopted as the anthem of the Korean exile government during the Japanese occupation.</p><p>It is distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s first official anthem. After the Gabo Reform of 1894 and the founding of the Independence association in 1896, about ten patriotic songs were written privately and their words printed in the Independence Newspaper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Emperor Gojong</a> then wanted an anthem as a symbol of the state: the German musician Franz von Eckert, who had led the band of the German Navy and worked for many years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, came to Korea in 1901 and completed the Korean Empire's national anthem, based on traditional Korean scales, on 1 July 1902; it was proclaimed on 15 August 1902. After the annexation of Korea by Japan in 1910, Japan's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a>&quot; became the official anthem and the Korean one was banned. After liberation it was not taken up again, because Eckert had taken part in composing &quot;Kimigayo&quot;.</p><p>Ahn Eak-tai's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegukga">Aegukga</a>&quot; was customarily called the national anthem, and it became the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a> after the government was founded on 15 August 1948, by custom more than by a formal act of designation. It has four verses; on most public occasions, such as baseball and football matches, only the first verse and the chorus are sung, and every KBO baseball game begins with it. In 2018 the music director Park In-young made a new arrangement with added brass and woodwind, keeping close to Ahn's original. Performing the song is prohibited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, where it is treated as a grave political crime.</p><p>Because Ahn Eak-tai died in 1965, the copyright in the music was to last until at least 2036, and in December 2003 a copyright holders' group sued two South Korean professional football clubs for playing it. On 16 March 2005 the composer's widow, Lolita Ahn, and her family gave up all rights to the song to the South Korean government, and it has since been in the public domain.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic song: the Encyclopedia of Korean Culture describes an aegukga as a song meant to awaken love of one's country. It is solemn and devoted rather than warlike.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Korean</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Korean</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">동해 물과 백두산이 마르고 닳도록,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Donghae mulgwa Baekdusani mareugo daltorok,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Until the waters of the East Sea and Mount Baekdu dry up and wear away,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">하느님이 보우하사 우리나라 만세.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Haneunimi bouhasa urinara manse.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may God protect us: long live our country.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">무궁화 삼천리 화려 강산,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Mugunghwa samcheolli hwaryeo gangsan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mugunghwa, three thousand ri of splendid rivers and mountains:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">대한 사람 대한으로 길이 보전하세.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Daehan saram Daehaneuro giri bojeonhase.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>people of Daehan, let us keep it long as Daehan.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">남산 위에 저 소나무 철갑을 두른 듯</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Namsan wie jeo sonamu cheolgabeul dureun deut</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Like that pine tree on top of Namsan, as if clad in iron armour,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">바람서리 불변함은 우리 기상일세.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">baramseori bulbyeonhameun uri gisangilse.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>unchanging through wind and frost: that is our spirit.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">가을 하늘 공활한데 높고 구름 없이</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Gaeul haneul gonghwalhande nopgo gureum eopsi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The autumn sky is wide and empty, high and without clouds;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">밝은 달은 우리 가슴 일편단심일세.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">balgeun dareun uri gaseum ilpyeondansimilse.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the bright moon is our heart, single and true.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">이 기상과 이 맘으로 충성을 다하여</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">I gisanggwa i mameuro chungseongeul dahayeo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With this spirit and this heart, giving all our loyalty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">괴로우나 즐거우나 나라 사랑하세.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">goerouna jeulgeouna nara saranghase.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in suffering or in joy, let us love our country.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Words of unknown authorship (1890s); the Korean text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegukga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Revised Romanization of Korean).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: the East Sea (Donghae) is the sea east of Korea; Baekdusan is the mountain on the border with China.</li><li>line 1.2: 'Haneunim', God or Heaven; 'bouhasa' is an archaic honorific form, 'may (He) protect'.</li><li>line 2.1: mugunghwa is the rose of Sharon (Hibiscus syriacus), Korea's flower; 'three thousand ri' is the traditional length of the country (a ri is about 400 m).</li><li>line 2.2: 'Daehan' (Great Han) is the country's name; literally 'Daehan people, as Daehan, long let us preserve (it)'.</li><li>line 3.1: Namsan, the mountain in the middle of Seoul.</li><li>line 4.2: 'ilpyeondansim', literally 'one piece of red heart': undivided loyalty.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
